---
import Comparison from "../../../components/studies/gravity-coil-form/Comparison.astro";
import Definitions from "../../../components/studies/gravity-coil-form/Definitions.astro";
import FleetSizing from "../../../components/studies/gravity-coil-form/FleetSizing.svelte";
import UnderRingComparison from "../../../components/studies/gravity-coil-form/UnderRingComparison.astro";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((s) => s.slug === "gravity-coil-form-2026-11-03");
const references = [
	{
		item: "ref-1a",
		label: "1A / Inner ribbon",
		origin: "02.8 · shaped faces",
		note: "Connected construction; established shape.",
		link: "depth",
	},
	{
		item: "ref-c",
		label: "C / One light",
		origin: "02.14 · C",
		note: "Promising later surface treatment.",
		link: "light",
	},
	{
		item: "ref-d",
		label: "D / Housed winding",
		origin: "02.14 · D",
		note: "Promising working-face / housing relationship.",
		link: "light",
	},
	{
		item: "well",
		label: "Potential Well",
		origin: "Current game",
		note: "Separated working bodies joined by yellow links.",
	},
	{
		item: "ion",
		label: "Light ion",
		origin: "Current game",
		note: "A dark body with broad facets and a defined end.",
	},
];
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<Definitions />
	<section>
		<h2 id="problem">01 / A whole component, belonging to the ship</h2>
		<p>
			A coil can be convincing over space and under a wing, yet read as a
			floating icon on the chassis. The segmented toroid, open centre and yellow
			connections are substantially decided. This study investigates the visual
			construction within that shape: how its surfaces and connections make it
			feel complete, and how those surfaces sit alongside the ship’s.
		</p>
		<p>
			The machinery belongs to the deliberately light ship or dark engine colour
			families; supports and connectors use safety yellow under upper-left
			starlight. The current game references provide context, but their
			placeholder treatments are not a finished colour target. Neither removing
			detail nor the promising C and D treatments settled the fit. Their
			strengths remain references, not explanations of the mismatch.
		</p>
		<p>
			The aim is sleek graphic machinery made with flat facets. The form rounds
			keep scale and placement fixed; 02.6 now explores size on the real ship
			slots, retaining M’s selected construction. FX, animation, mechanics,
			balance and production implementation are outside this study, as are
			backing plates, pedestals, isometric presentation and literal realism.
		</p>
		<p class="small">
			Fresh cycle ·
			<a href="/lab/studies/gravity-coil-2026-11-03"
				>the stopped study remains separate</a
			>. The coil references below are preserved vector snapshots, with their
			original treatments intact.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2>02 / Surfaces that make an assembly</h2>
		<details>
			<summary id="references">
				02.1 / Chosen references in game context
			</summary>
			<p>
				Read across the same two scales: the equipment alone, then the same
				equipment on a fully fitted Fighter. The Light ion column shows the
				Fighter with engines only. The reference drawings are promising designs;
				the production equipment still includes placeholder colour treatments.
			</p>
			<!-- biome-ignore lint/a11y/noNoninteractiveTabindex: Keyboard access to the horizontally scrollable fixed-scale references. -->
			<section class="reference-scroll" aria-label="References" tabindex="0">
				<div class="reference-grid">
					{references.map((ref) => (
						<figure>
							<figcaption>
								<strong>{ref.label}</strong>
								<span>{ref.origin}</span>
							</figcaption>
							<svg
								width="180"
								height="180"
								viewBox="-15 -15 30 30"
								role="img"
								aria-label={`${ref.label}, alone, 6×`}
							>
								<use href={`#form-${ref.item}`} />
							</svg>
							<span class="scale">Alone · 6×</span>
							<svg
								width="160"
								height="180"
								viewBox="-40 -52 80 90"
								role="img"
								aria-label={`${ref.label}, Fighter, 2×`}
							>
								<use
									href={
										ref.item === "ion"
											? "#form-ship-fighter"
											: `#form-${ref.item}-fighter-over`
									}
								/>
							</svg>
							<span class="scale">Fighter · 2×</span>
							<svg
								data-native
								width="80"
								height="90"
								viewBox="-40 -52 80 90"
								role="img"
								aria-label={`${ref.label}, Fighter, 1×`}
							>
								<use
									href={
										ref.item === "ion"
											? "#form-ship-fighter"
											: `#form-${ref.item}-fighter-over`
									}
								/>
							</svg>
							<span class="scale">Fighter · 1×</span>
							<p>{ref.note}</p>
							{ref.link && (
								<a href={`/lab/studies/gravity-coil-2026-11-03#${ref.link}`}>
									Source drawing ↗
								</a>
							)}
						</figure>
					))}
				</div>
			</section>
			<p class="small">
				All engines are Light ion; every available engine slot is fitted. 1×
				means one ship drawing unit per CSS pixel at 100% browser zoom, matching
				the builder’s native scale. Flight uses 0.65 of that scale. Equipment
				stays at its fitted size: coil artwork ×0.5; Potential Well at its
				production ×0.55. Enlargements multiply the entire scene uniformly.
				Narrow screens scroll; the drawings never shrink to fit.
			</p>
			<p>
				Two relationships are worth exploring:
				<strong>the direction of adjoining faces</strong>, where an outer slope
				and an aperture wall describe different sides of the same volume; and
				<strong>the division between working material and its support</strong>,
				where a dark winding can sit within a light saddle. Either may help the
				coil belong. These drawings can compare those possibilities; they cannot
				yet establish why the earlier fit failed.
			</p>
		</details>
		<details>
			<summary id="construction">
				02.2 / Two constructions inside the chosen shape — E retained
			</summary>
			<p>
				The eight sectors, their outer contour, the open centre and the yellow
				inner connection stay. The first construction gives the light machinery
				broad creased faces. The second exposes dark working faces between light
				saddles. This is a comparison of whole constructions, not a palette
				selector or a set of small tuning variations.
			</p>
			<h4>C / Preserved reference</h4>
			<p>The promising 02.14 C, unchanged, in the same views used below.</p>
			<Comparison item="ref-c" label="C / Preserved reference" />
			<h4>E / Creased sector shells</h4>
			<p>
				Each light sector folds over a crest: the outside falls toward space,
				the inside toward the opening. The yellow ribbon joins the sectors from
				within. This tests whether clear, opposing faces can give the existing
				light assembly volume without a separate enclosing rim. The risk is that
				a ring of light facets still reads as a badge on the hull.
			</p>
			<Comparison item="shell" label="E / Creased sector shells" />
			<h4>F / Dark windings in light saddles</h4>
			<p>
				The same sectors become dark working bodies, captured at their ends and
				inner shoulders by light saddles. Yellow remains the continuous
				connection. This tests a more explicit separation of working material
				and support, while keeping both within the established silhouette. The
				risk is that the saddles become decorative borders, or their
				construction disappears at 1×.
			</p>
			<Comparison item="saddle" label="F / Dark windings in light saddles" />
			<p class="small">
				Static vector proposals. Fighter on-chassis, Wasp on-chassis and Wasp
				under-wing use fixed mounts and unchanged production hull geometry. Wasp
				on-chassis is a comparison fixture; its normal hull fittings sit below
				the hull. E is retained for continued exploration; no finished
				construction is accepted.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Review:</strong>
				E is promising: the first version that feels as though it belongs to the
				game. Its connection to the ship when on top remains unresolved. The
				original narrow edge facet is worth testing against E’s nearly equal
				face widths. Dark tones may be under-used, but can also make the result
				icon-like. F has not been selected for development.
			</p>
		</details>
		<details>
			<summary id="shoulder">02.3 / A broad face and a narrow shoulder</summary>
			<p>
				E’s faceted character is the reference to protect. G tests the original
				proportion: a narrow outside shoulder and one broad face toward the
				aperture. The outline, yellow connections, inner dark return and light
				assignments are unchanged. This isolates the face proportion before
				changing what the darks do.
			</p>
			<h4>E / Retained reference</h4>
			<p>
				Promising game belonging; connection to the hull still unresolved.
				Preserved unchanged.
			</p>
			<Comparison item="shell" label="E / Retained reference" />
			<h4>G / Narrow shoulder</h4>
			<p>
				The small outside facet now turns the edge; the broad inward face
				carries the sector. This is still E’s peaked ring, with the crest nearer
				its outside edge. The light remains directional rather than giving every
				segment a uniform highlight.
			</p>
			<Comparison item="narrow" label="G / Narrow shoulder" />
			<p>
				<strong>Reading the comparison:</strong>
				G makes the inward-facing surface dominant, so its largest bright faces
				sit toward the lower right, opposite the hull’s broad highlight. That
				follows this proposed peaked volume; it does not establish that the
				light is wrong. The next comparison separates that volume from a shallow
				crown, and gives dark material a larger role.
			</p>
		</details>
		<details>
			<summary id="dark-faces">02.4 / Dark bodies or dark returns?</summary>
			<p>
				H makes the broad sector bodies dark, leaving the lit shoulders and
				yellow connections to articulate them. I keeps broad light crowns and
				gives the strong darks to their narrow, steep returns. These are
				different constructions within the same contour. Neither puts a
				continuous dark frame around the coil, adds a backing surface or paints
				a shadow onto the hull.
			</p>
			<h4>H / Dark sector bodies</h4>
			<p>
				The engine-coloured body is the main mass. Its narrow shoulder catches
				light in the ship palette, then falls into charcoal on the unlit side.
				Unlike F’s saddles, a bright frame does not enclose each dark face. This
				tests a coil that belongs beside the engines through its working bodies,
				with yellow carrying the connection. The risk is that its volume becomes
				too quiet over space.
			</p>
			<Comparison item="dark-shell" label="H / Dark sector bodies" />
			<h4>I / Shallow crowns with steep returns</h4>
			<p>
				A broad, shallow upper face stays in the light family. Narrow outside
				and aperture walls carry the stronger tonal change, and a sloped end
				meets the inner connection. This tests whether sharing the hull’s broad
				light surfaces helps the coil feel fitted, while the darks describe its
				depth. The risk is a flatter, less distinctive winding.
			</p>
			<Comparison item="crown" label="I / Shallow crowns with steep returns" />
			<p>
				<strong>Review finding:</strong>
				These experiments were useful, but reshaping is not the direction to
				continue. E remains the chosen reference for further exploration. The
				shallow crown is not carried forward. The next step keeps E intact and
				investigates a separate ring beneath it.
			</p>
			<p class="small">
				These are alternative constructions, not successive approvals. E remains
				the reviewed reference. All context views retain the same production
				hulls, fully fitted Light ion engines, mounting positions and scales. 1×
				remains one drawing unit per CSS pixel; narrow screens scroll without
				shrinking the drawings.
			</p>
		</details>
		<h3 id="under-ring">02.5 / E on an engine-coloured ring</h3>
		<p>
			The original E sits unchanged above each proposed ring. J, K and L
			increase the exposed rim from narrow to medium to wide. M keeps its ring
			inside the coil’s outline, visible only through the sector gaps. That last
			comparison separates visible connection beneath the sectors from the
			effect of a surrounding rim.
		</p>
		<p>
			The new layer uses the Light ion’s charcoal tones, with a shallow
			upper-left-lit edge. It is an open ring, not a filled backing plate: the
			centre and yellow connections remain E’s. The hypothesis is that a
			continuous lower body could make the equipment feel seated on the hull. A
			broad dark border could also strengthen the icon reading; neither outcome
			is assumed.
		</p>
		<UnderRingComparison />
		<p id="decision">
			<strong>Selected: M / Inset ring.</strong>
			E’s creased sector shells, open centre and yellow connections remain
			unchanged. A continuous engine-coloured ring sits beneath the
			construction, inside its outer silhouette, and is visible through the
			gaps. No exposed outer rim is carried forward. This settles the
			construction and size: 100% was selected in 02.6. The shells and inset
			ring remain unchanged.
		</p>
		<p class="small">
			Read across each row: E, narrow, medium, wide, inset. All isolated views
			are 6×; all enlarged ship views in this comparison are 2×. Each has a true
			1× sample beneath it. Ship geometry, all fitted Light ion engines, mounts
			and coil size stay fixed. Only the underlying ring changes. The preceding
			round’s ship views remain labelled 3×.
		</p>
		<h3 id="sizing">02.6 / M across the fleet — sizing tool</h3>
		<p>
			The shared slider established M’s size on all four production hulls. Light
			ions fill every engine-compatible slot, including the universal aft slots.
			M fills every remaining equipment slot at its actual mount position. 100%
			matches the selected reference above. The preserved tool now holds that
			size fixed.
		</p>
		<p>
			Compare the space around neighbouring fittings, how much of the coil
			remains visible, and its presence at native and flight scale. Normal hull
			layering is the starting point; the above-hull option exposes concealed
			parts for comparison. <strong>100% is the final selected size.</strong>
			The size control is fixed to preserve that decision.
		</p>
		<FleetSizing client:load />
		<p class="small">
			Mounting context is preserved as reviewed. A subsequent, separate Fighter
			mount decision moved its equipment slots from (2, 0) and (-10, 0) to (37,
			0) and (-1, 0). The
			<a href="/lab/utility-artwork#gravity-coil">production fleet view</a>
			uses the current positions with the same accepted coil size.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2 id="readiness">03 / Ready at the selected size</h2>
		<p>
			<strong>M / Inset ring at 100% is accepted.</strong>
			The shared renderer can reproduce its flat filled faces directly. The
			artwork scale is 0.5, inside the ship’s scene scale; 0.65 flight scale
			does not change that relationship. No new dependency or effect system is
			needed.
		</p>
		<p>
			The conversion preserves E’s shell faces and the inset ring beneath them,
			their paint order, the open aperture and yellow connections. Nose-up study
			coordinates become local +x forward; RGB strings become equivalent hex
			colours for the validated shared face format. The study definitions remain
			independent references for geometry, colour and scale comparisons.
		</p>
		<p>
			The final delivery also includes an explicitly requested separate
			<strong>Gravity coil</strong>
			fitting. It uses the existing catalog, loadout persistence and
			builder/flight renderer. Its provisional mass is 10, matching Loading
			coil. Collection is inactive: it adds no charging, storage, thrust or
			energy draw. Loading coil retains its solar behavior.
		</p>
		<p>
			The fleet sizing comparison supported the 100% decision across all four
			hulls. Wasp and Scout retain below-hull fittings; Fighter and Corvette
			retain above-hull fittings. Concealment and overlap follow the actual
			mounts. Static-form acceptance does not define future collection behavior,
			FX or animation.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2 id="scope">04 / Accepted delivery</h2>
		<p>
			<strong
				>No change to the art direction in <code>docs/design.md</code> is
				proposed.</strong
			>
			The feature contract belongs in
			<code>docs/history/ship-builder.md</code>. The study is accepted; its
			original references and all useful comparisons remain available here.
		</p>
		<h3>1 / Shared static artwork · small</h3>
		<p>
			Replace the earlier <code>gravity</code> artwork with M and give it an
			explicit default scale of 0.5. Use the shared face schema and painter in
			Canvas and SVG views. Preserve the other utilities’ sizes and the frozen
			study references. Fidelity checks compare every face and colour against M.
		</p>
		<h3>2 / Separate selectable fitting · small, after slice 1</h3>
		<p>
			Add <code>gravity-coil</code> to compatible equipment and universal slots,
			with a catalog preview and the shared artwork in builder and flight.
			Retain saved builds and starter fleets. Clearly describe collection as
			inactive; verify that fitting it adds only its provisional mass, with no
			collection, capacity or energy draw. This hookup was explicitly added to
			the delivery after the static-form decision.
		</p>
		<h3>3 / Mounted fidelity · small, after slice 2</h3>
		<p>
			Use real loadouts on all four hulls, every equipment mount fitted and
			Light ions in every engine-compatible slot. Compare shared drawings at 3×,
			true 1× and 0.65 flight scale against the 100% reference. Verify
			selection, persistence, test flight and return in the actual builder.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Implemented locally:</strong>
			<a href="/lab/utility-artwork#gravity-coil"
				>shared artwork and fleet views</a
			>
			· <a href="/hangar">selectable Gravity coil in the ship builder</a>.
			Collection mechanics, balancing, FX and animation remain outside this
			delivery. Publication is separate.
		</p>
		<p class="small">
			<a href="/lab/reviews/gravity-coil-form-2026-11-03"
				>Study-quality review and evidence limits</a
			>
			· separate from feasibility and implementation scope.
		</p>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>
<style>
.small {
	font-size: 13px;
	color: var(--color-text-muted);
}
.reference-scroll {
	overflow-x: auto;
	margin-block: 24px;
	border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
}
.reference-scroll:focus-visible {
	outline: 2px solid var(--color-action);
	outline-offset: 3px;
}
.reference-grid {
	display: grid;
	grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 190px);
	width: 950px;
	margin-inline: auto;
	padding-block: 20px;
}
figure {
	margin: 0;
	text-align: center;
	padding-inline: 5px;
}
figcaption strong {
	display: block;
	font-size: 14px;
}
figcaption span,
.scale {
	display: block;
	font: 12px var(--font-family-ui);
	color: var(--color-text-muted);
}
figcaption {
	min-height: 48px;
}
figure svg {
	display: block;
	max-width: none;
	margin-inline: auto;
}
figure p {
	font-size: 13px;
	line-height: 1.6;
	padding: 8px;
	min-height: 86px;
}
figure a {
	font-size: 12px;
}
</style>
